            <ArticleTitle>Granulomatous Angiitis of the Central Nervous System Associated with Hodgkin's Lymphoma: Case Report and Literature Review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e5-e8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1052-3057(17)30450-0</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jstrokecerebrovasdis.2017.08.029</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Granulomatous angiitis of the central nervous system (GACNS) is a rare cerebrovascular disorder. It usually presents with multifocal neurologic symptoms symptoms including stroke, encephalopathy, and headache. A limited number of case reports describe neurological deficits resulting from GACNS as the manifesting symptoms of Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL). We describe the case of a patient with neurological symptoms from GACNS that led to the diagnosis of HL, as well as a literature review focusing on the association between GACNS and HL.</AbstractText>
